Ladybird compares and registers asynchronous atomic waits under the same
lock as blocking JavaScript and WebAssembly waits. A mismatch returns
“not-equal”, and a matching zero-timeout wait returns “timed-out”. Other
waits return an intrinsic promise and join the shared FIFO waiter list.

V8 performs this comparison before zero-timeout handling in its
FutexEmulation::WaitAsync function. SpiderMonkey also follows that
order in AtomicsWaitAsyncCriticalSection, as does JavaScriptCore in
WaiterListManager::waitAsyncImpl. All three serialize registration with
notification; Ladybird extends its AK waiter list to do this.

Ladybird resolves a notified promise on its owning agent. Same-agent
notification resolves during Atomics.notify(), as NotifyWaiter requires.
SpiderMonkey follows this order in js::atomics_notify_impl. V8 uses
FutexEmulation::NotifyAsyncWaiter to post a task, and JavaScriptCore
uses WaiterListManager::notifyWaiterImpl to schedule deferred work even
for a same-agent notification. A Chrome probe confirmed the resulting
microtask-order difference. Ladybird follows the current EcmaScript
algorithm and SpiderMonkey for this case.

For cross-agent notifications, Ladybird sends only native completion
identifiers between threads, then queues a JavaScript-engine task on
the receiving global. Finite waits use the HTML timeout machinery,
with internal timer cancellation kept separate from clearTimeout() and
clearInterval(). The standalone hosts drain completions between promise-
job checkpoints and sleep until notification or the next deadline.

In V8, completion resolves the promise on its owning thread using the
FutexEmulation::ResolveAsyncWaiterPromise function. SpiderMonkey
uses WaitAsyncNotifyTask::resolve for this, and JavaScriptCore uses
WaiterListManager::notifyWaiterImpl. Their timeout and notification
paths select one completion and cancel any unnecessary timeout work.
Ladybird uses the waiter-list lock for that selection and clears a
completed wait’s host timer.

Ladybird removes registrations before destroying an agent, and drops
waits whose realm is collected. A reachable realm retains its wait
resolvers and buffers through ephemeron edges; the wait list itself
holds no GC pointers. Keeping the buffer alive until completion protects
the address used by the process-wide waiter list. V8 and JavaScriptCore
also remove waits for dead contexts or agents, and can release
unreferenced backing storage earlier. SpiderMonkey handles a dead
backing buffer separately in WaitAsyncNotifyTask.

Ladybird returns a module evaluation promise to its standalone host
when a top-level await suspends evaluation. The host runs async-wait
jobs and checks the promise for rejection afterward. Evaluation from
a promise reaction returns without blocking the other queued jobs.
The browser uses its own module and event-loop integration.

V8 returns the evaluation promise to d8 in Shell::ExecuteModule. The
shell processes host tasks before inspecting rejection. SpiderMonkey
returns JS::ModuleEvaluate through ModuleLoader::loadAndExecute. In
JavaScriptCore, runWithOptions attaches completion handlers to the
loadAndEvaluateModule promise and drains microtasks outside it. Each
host observes completion rather than requiring immediate settlement.

Chrome completes a top-level wait with timed-out after its deadline.
Node produces the same result when a timer keeps its event loop alive.
A waitAsync promise alone doesn’t keep Node alive. Its lifetime
policy differs from the standalone Ladybird async-wait driver, which
waits for registered async waits when running a script from a file.

Ladybird schedules finite Atomics.waitAsync waits in worklets with
internal one-shot timers. The worklet owns the Core timers directly,
traces their callbacks, cancels them when notification completes a wait,
and stops any remaining timers when the global is finalized. Completion
still queues a JavaScript engine task for the original realm. No
setTimeout or setInterval API is exposed.

HTML’s HostEnqueueTimeoutJob passes the realm’s global to run steps
after a timeout. That algorithm only defines Window and
WorkerGlobalScope globals, leaving a worklet integration gap. Creating a
worklet realm does not remove Atomics.waitAsync, and the EcmaScript
DoWait restriction on agents that cannot suspend applies only to
synchronous waits. Ladybird marks its internal worklet timeout
implementation AD-HOC to identify the gap.

Chrome runs the reported AudioWorklet module without crashing. Inside
the worklet, Atomics.waitAsync is a function and setTimeout is
undefined. A second test observes timed-out and ok results for a finite
wait and a notified wait. Blink’s V8 FutexEmulation::WaitAsync schedules
an AsyncWaiterTimeoutTask through the isolate task runner; this does not
require a DOM timer method. WebKit’s WaiterListManager::waitAsyncImpl
likewise schedules an internal RunLoop::DispatchTimer.

Gecko differs: Its WorkletThread DelayedDispatchToEventLoop returns
false. SpiderMonkey’s AtomicsObject waitAsync path delegates delayed
work to that hook. This source inspection does not establish a
successful Firefox worklet timeout. Ladybird follows the measured Chrome
behavior, using its own timer implementation.
